# The accepted residual for scripts/bandit-audit.py (issue #962).
#
# ONE LINE PER (file, rule), with the COUNT and the issue tracking it. This
# file is the mechanism that replaced codex-power-pack's inherited
# `--skip B104,B108,B310,B602`, and the difference is the whole of #962:
#
#   * a --skip turns a rule off across the whole tree, forever, invisibly.
#     Measured here, it removes 10 of this tree's 11 MEDIUM+ findings.
#   * a line below is a COUNT BASELINE for one (file, rule). A new site in the
#     same file is a finding; a new site in a fresh file is a finding; and a
#     recorded site that gets FIXED turns the gate red until its line comes
#     out, so a suppression cannot outlive the count it records.
#
# WHAT A COUNT DOES NOT ESTABLISH, said here because a reader of this file is
# the person most likely to rely on it: it is not a per-site identity. Fix one
# accepted site and add a different one in the same file, and the count is
# unchanged and the gate stays green - the REPLACEMENT is invisible. That is a
# deliberate bound, not an oversight: a line number is invalidated by every
# edit above it and a snippet hash by every reformat, and a ledger that reddens
# on unrelated edits gets switched off (ADR 0009). Whether an accepted site is
# still the same site is a question for review of the diff that moved it and
# for #1113, and `tests/test_bandit_audit.py` pins the blind spot so it stays a
# committed fact rather than a claim in a comment.
#
# Format: finding <path> <test-id> <count> <issue>
# Whole-line comments only - `#` also opens every issue reference on a record.
#
# Every line here is dispositioned by #1113. The 136 LOW-severity findings are
# BELOW this gate's threshold, are counted and printed on every run, and are
# tracked by #1114 - they are deliberately not listed here.

